<div class="wrap page-head"><p class="kicker">Founder and chairman</p><h1>Jack Figel</h1><p class="lede">Reader, publisher, and for thirty years the organizer of the conversation between East and West that the official dialogues could not hold on their own.</p></div>
<section class="band" style="padding-top:1.5rem">
  <div class="wrap bio-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="dropcap">Jack Figel founded the Orientale Lumen Conferences in Washington, DC, in 1997 and has chaired every one since. He is president of Eastern Christian Publications in Fairfax, Virginia, the national chairman of the Society of St. John Chrysostom in the United States, and founder and chairman of the Orientale Lumen Foundation, the nonprofit that grew out of both.</p>
      <p>The idea was simple and, at the time, unusual: put Eastern Orthodox, Eastern Catholic, and Roman Catholic Christians in one room for several days, let them hear the same lectures and attend each other's liturgies, and see what friendships form. The official Catholic-Orthodox dialogue meets behind closed doors; Orientale Lumen meets in public, with lay people and with the Eastern Catholics who are seldom represented anywhere else. Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew called it an effort that "may be grass roots" but is "at the same time rooted in heaven." Cardinal Kurt Koch, then president of the Pontifical Council for Promoting Christian Unity, sent a personal message to the 2019 conference.</p>
      <p>Jack has had eight private audiences with four popes, John Paul II, Benedict XVI, Francis, and Leo XIV, and more than twenty meetings with Ecumenical Patriarch Bartholomew, who greets him as "Mr. Jack the Book Publisher." He has led the Foundation's pilgrims into those audiences, taken three conferences to Constantinople at the Patriarch's invitation, including a day at the Halki Theological Academy, and represented the Foundation at the Vatican's 2017 conference on nuclear disarmament. In 2023 the National Council of Churches gave him the Miller Award for Excellence in Ecumenical Leadership. In July 2026, at the thirtieth conference, the Apostolic Nuncio presented him with the medal of Pope Leo XIV's first year, sent by the Pope himself.</p>
      <p>The Washington Theological Consortium's annual lecture on ecumenism, the <a class="textlink" href="https://washtheocon.org/for-the-public/figel-address/">Figel Address on Ecumenical Dialogue</a>, is named for him and paired each year with the Consortium's Ecumenism Award. Bishop John Michael Botean of the Romanian Greek Catholic Eparchy of St. George in Canton has been a friend of the conferences since their early years and spoke at the thirtieth.</p>
      <p>For the Foundation's thirtieth anniversary he announced its next direction: discussions with the Likoudis Legacy Foundation about an educational and ecumenical institute offering accredited advanced studies in ecumenical theology, to equip a younger generation to receive and contribute to the agreed statements of the dialogue, Ravenna, Chieti, and Alexandria among them.</p>
      <p>He is a tonsured Reader in the Byzantine Catholic Church and lives in Fairfax, Virginia.</p>
